  3. [1m[38;5;153mtotal[39m and [38;5;153mcreatedAt[39m cross the handler boundary unvalidated[0m ([38;5;153msrc/handlers.js:36[39m). [38;5;153mid[39m gets a strict regex; the other
     two get nothing. [38;5;153mcreatedAt[39m is taken verbatim from the client, so orders can be arbitrarily backdated or saved with
     no timestamp. Notably [38;5;153mnowIso[39m exists at [38;5;153msrc/util.js:3[39m and — I grepped — is called from nowhere, suggesting a
     server-generated timestamp was the intent.

  [1mMinor (reviewer's, not individually verified)[0m

  [38;5;153mcreateOrderHandler[39m has no [38;5;153mbody = {}[39m default so a missing body throws [38;5;153mTypeError[39m instead of 400; [38;5;153mpage[39m/[38;5;153msize[39m are unbounded
  (negative page, client-chosen page size); response has no [38;5;153mtotal[39m/[38;5;153mhasMore[39m; [38;5;153mwithRetry[39m wraps an in-memory [38;5;153mslice[39m that
  cannot fail, so it's currently inert; [38;5;153msaveOrder[39m doesn't check for duplicate ids; tests share mutable [38;5;153mstore.orders[39m
  state and are order-dependent; the [38;5;153mconfig.json[39m externalization is unrelated to this commit's stated purpose.

